# Nobody complies: intention-to-treat versus the effect of treatment
subtitle: "What a lottery's winners-versus-losers gap measures when most winners never enrol, why comparing enrollees with the rest breaks the experiment, and how dividing by take-up recovers the effect of coverage."
tags: ["intention to treat","noncompliance","randomized trials","medicaid"]
teaches: ["methods/intention-to-treat","methods/noncompliance","methods/instrumental-variables"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow"}
next: {"title":"Instrumental variables and the LATE theorem","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/causal-inference-in-economics-evidence-from-health-and-health-care/instrumental-variables-and-the-late-theorem.cast"}

## Winning is not enrolling
voice: male
level: advanced
vars: {"n_cov":0,"n_un":{"expr":"100 - n_cov"}}
sources:
    - id: finkelstein2012
      title: 'The Oregon Health Insurance Experiment: Evidence from the First Year'
      authors: Finkelstein, Taubman, Wright, Bernstein, Gruber, Newhouse, Allen, Baicker and the Oregon Health Study Group, Quarterly Journal of Economics
      year: 2012
      finding: About 90,000 people signed up for a lottery for about 10,000 Medicaid places in 2008; about 30,000 were selected. Only about 60% of those selected sent back applications, about half of those were ineligible (mainly on income), and about 30% of those selected enrolled.

    text h_win "Drawn" x 230 y 655 font_size 38 hidden true
    math z_win tex "Z_i = 1" size 36 colors.Z_i #4f8a4b x 380 y 655 hidden true
    text h_lose "Not drawn" x 690 y 655 font_size 38 hidden true
    math z_lose tex "Z_i = 0" size 36 colors.Z_i #4f8a4b x 870 y 655 hidden true
    population win cites ["finkelstein2012"] fit.h 320 fit.w 420 fit.x 50 fit.y 300 colors.covered #2f6b8f colors.uncovered #8f887c count 100 states.uncovered 100 states.covered 0 order rows legend false labels.covered covered labels.uncovered "not covered" bind {"states.covered":"n_cov","states.uncovered":"100 - n_cov"} hidden true
    population lose fit.h 320 fit.w 420 fit.x 530 fit.y 300 colors.uncovered #8f887c count 100 states.uncovered 100 order rows legend false labels.uncovered "not covered" hidden true
    text leg_win "{n_cov:0} covered, {n_un:0} not" x 260 y 282 font_size 30 hidden true
    text leg_lose "none covered" x 740 y 282 font_size 30 hidden true
    scale guess x 120 y 140 width 760 label "winners covered" min 0 max 100 value 30 hidden true
    text y_win "46 have a clinic" x 260 y 242 font_size 36 hidden true
    text y_lose "34 have a clinic" x 740 y 242 font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "Winning is not enrolling"

Oregon once held a lottery for health insurance, and most of its winners never  (@draw lose leg_lose@)ended up insured. So what does comparing winners with losers actually measure?
    draw win leg_win

It began in 2008, when the state could afford about ten thousand more adults on Medicaid, and some nin (@draw h_lose@)ety thousand put their names down.
    draw h_win

So it drew about thirty thousand names, a (@draw z_win@)nd each winner was invited to apply. Call (@draw z_lose@) the draw Z: one for a winner, zero for everyone else.

Here are a hundred winners and a hundred losers, standing in for the thousands.
    highlight win

    draw guess

    ask "How many of a hundred winners got Medicaid? Click the line." store g on guess stage own voice b
        right: About thirty. You said {g}, close enough.
        wrong: You said {g}; it was only about thirty.

About thirty. Six in ten winners sent back the forms, and about half of those turned out to earn too much, or failed another rule.
    animate n_cov 30 duration 2.5

    erase guess

B: So the lottery handed out invitations, not insurance.
    highlight win_uncovered

It did. The draw decides Z, the offer. Coverage, call it D, follows it for fewer than one winner in three, and in our example for no loser at all.
    highlight win_covered

Now take an outcome, call it Y: having a regular clinic to go to. Our numbers are mad (@highlight y_win@)e up, but close in size to Oregon's.
    draw y_win

Among a hundred winners, forty-six have one.  (@draw y_lose@)Among a hundred losers, thirty-four.

Winners minus losers, everyone counted as drawn: twelve percentage points. That is the intention-to-treat effect.
    text itt_lab "Intention to treat" #4f8a4b x 260 y 168 font_size 34
    math itt tex "E[Y_i \\mid Z_i = 1] - E[Y_i \\mid Z_i = 0] = 12" size 34 colors {"12":"#4f8a4b","Z_i":"#4f8a4b"} x 690 y 168

B: But seventy of those winners never got covered. Isn't the twelve simply wrong?
    point at.ref win_uncovered gesture circle

Not wrong: diluted. Only chance decided who won, so the whole twelve points is caused by winning.
    mark itt_box around itt_lab itt kind box

It is the effect of the offer, averaged over everyone who got one, most of whom never used it.
    highlight win_uncovered y_win

B: Then if only thirty people moved, their own gain must be far bigger than twelve.
    highlight win_covered

That is the number we are after. And there is a tempting shortcut to it, right here among the winners.
    highlight win_covered effect circle

## The tempting shortcut
voice: male
level: advanced
sources:
    - id: finkelstein2012
      title: 'The Oregon Health Insurance Experiment: Evidence from the First Year'
      authors: Finkelstein, Taubman, Wright, Bernstein, Gruber, Newhouse, Allen, Baicker and the Oregon Health Study Group, Quarterly Journal of Economics
      year: 2012
      finding: Compliers (those who enrolled because they were selected) were somewhat older, more likely white, in worse health and of lower socioeconomic status than the lottery population. Observational estimates in the same data, which ignore the lottery, show larger effects on use of care and opposite-signed health effects (insurance appearing to worsen health), which the authors attribute to people in poorer health selecting into coverage.

    text h_grp "Winners" x 190 y 670 font_size 44 hidden true
    math h_y1 tex Y_i(1) size 48 colors {"Y_i(1)":"#2f6b8f"} x 560 y 670 hidden true
    math h_y0 tex Y_i(0) size 48 colors {"Y_i(0)":"#b5482e"} x 780 y 670 hidden true
    math h_t tex \tau_i size 48 colors {"\\tau_i":"#8a5fa8"} x 990 y 670 hidden true
    path rule_h thick #3d3833 points [[40,625],[1085,625]] hidden true
    text r1_lab "Enrolled · 30" #2f6b8f cites ["finkelstein2012"] x 190 y 565 font_size 44 hidden true
    text r1_y1 "60" #2f6b8f x 560 y 565 font_size 56 hidden true
    text r1_q "?" #8f887c x 780 y 565 font_size 62 hidden true
    text r1_y0 "20" #b5482e x 780 y 565 font_size 56 style.opacity 0.6 hidden true
    text r1_t "40" #8a5fa8 x 990 y 565 font_size 56 hidden true
    text r2_lab "Stayed out · 70" x 190 y 465 font_size 44 hidden true
    text r2_y1 "–" #8f887c x 560 y 465 font_size 56 hidden true
    text r2_y0 "40" #b5482e x 780 y 465 font_size 56 hidden true
    path rule_a #8f887c points [[40,400],[1085,400]] style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    math gap_eq tex "60 - 40 = 20" size 56 x 562 y 280 hidden true
    figure table aspect 3:2 members ["h_grp","h_y1","h_y0","h_t","rule_h","r1_lab","r1_y1","r1_q","r1_y0","r1_t","r2_lab","r2_y1","r2_y0","rule_a","gap_eq"] hidden true

    card title "The tempting shortcut"

Here is the shortcut. Among the winners we can see exactly who enrolled, so why not compare them with the winners who stayed out?
    draw h_grp rule_h r1_lab r2_lab

Write Y of one for a person's outcome (@draw h_y0@) with coverage, and Y of zero without. Sixty percent of (@draw r1_y1 r2_y1@) the enrolled have a regular clinic; forty percent (@draw r2_y0@) of those who stayed out.
    draw h_y1

B: A twenty-point gap, and still inside a randomized experiment. What could be wrong with that?
    draw rule_a gap_eq

The lottery decided who was drawn. It did not decide who enrolled: the winners decided that themselves.
    highlight r1_lab r2_lab

And in Oregon, those who enrolled because they won were older, poorer and in worse health than the lottery's entrants as a whole, so worse off than the winners who stayed out.
    highlight r1_lab effect underline

    quiz "The enrolled were poorer and sicker than the winners who stayed out, and less likely to have a regular clinic on their own. So the twenty-point gap is…" voice b
        intro: Before we look inside the table:
        right: Smaller: without coverage the enrolled would have been further behind the others, so comparing them with the others understates what coverage did.
        wrong: The draw was random; enrolling was not. Ask where the enrolled would have stood without coverage.
        + Smaller than what coverage did for them
        * Bigger than what coverage did for them
        * Exactly what coverage did, since the draw was random

Fill in the cell the data never show: the enrolled winners' shar (@highlight r1_q effect circle@)e with a clinic, had they stayed uncovered.
    draw r1_q

    erase r1_q

Poorer and sicker, say they would have had one only twenty pe (@highlight r1_y0 r2_y0@)rcent of the time. That twenty is made up, like all our example's numbers.
    draw r1_y0

So coverage lifted them from twenty to sixty. That gap is the effect, tau: forty points.
    draw h_t r1_t

B: Then the shortcut's twenty finds only half of it. Where did the other twenty go?
    point at.ref gap_eq gesture underline

Split the gap in two, by adding and taking away the enrolled group's twenty.
    morph gap_eq tex "60 - 40 = (60 - 20) + (20 - 40)"

The first piece, sixty minus twenty, is what cove (@highlight r1_y1 r1_y0 r1_t effect circle@)rage did for the enrolled: forty points.
    highlight gap_eq part "(60 - 20)" effect box

The second compares two groups who are bot (@highlight r1_y0 r2_y0 effect circle@)h uncovered, so it measures who they are, not what they got: minus twenty. That is selection bias.
    highlight gap_eq part "(20 - 40)" effect box

Forty points of effect, minus twenty points of selection, and the shortcut shows twenty.
    morph gap_eq tex "20 = 40 + (-20)"

B: So once we split the winners by what they chose, the lottery stops protecting us?
    highlight r1_lab r2_lab effect circle

Completely. Oregon's team saw it in their own data: compare the covered with the uncovered, and insurance even seemed to make people's health worse.
    highlight r1_y1 r2_y0

The draw's two groups were never split by choice, so on average each holds th (@highlight r1_lab r2_lab effect circle@)e same mix of people. That is where the honest forty is hiding.
    erase gap_eq

## Dividing out the dilution
