sources:
    - id: finkelstein2012
      title: 'The Oregon Health Insurance Experiment: Evidence from the First Year'
      authors: Finkelstein, Taubman, Wright, Bernstein, Gruber, Newhouse, Allen, Baicker and the Oregon Health Study Group, Quarterly Journal of Economics
      year: 2012
      finding: 'Survey sample, about a year after the lottery: selection raised the chance of ever being on Medicaid by 29 percentage points (control mean 13.5%). Having a usual place of clinic-based care: intention-to-treat +9.9 points (control mean 49.9%), effect of coverage (IV) +33.9 points. Self-reported health good, very good or excellent: +3.9 and +13.3 points (control mean 54.8%). All p < 0.0001.'

    math m tex "12 = 0.30 \\times {\\bar{\\tau}} + 0.70 \\times 0" size 54 colors {"12":"#4f8a4b","{\\bar{\\tau}}":"#8a5fa8"} x 500 y 610 hidden true
