    text tx50 "50" #8f887c at.data [50,-3.5] font_size 28 hidden true
    text ty20 "20" #8f887c at.data [-4,20] font_size 28 hidden true
    text ty40 "40" #8f887c at.data [-4,40] font_size 28 hidden true
    text readout "+{s:0} covered → +{itt:0} points" #4f8a4b at.data [24,48] font_size 32 hidden true
    text or_itt "offer 9.9 ± 1.6" #4f8a4b at.data [28,24] cites ["finkelstein2012"] font_size 32 hidden true
    text or_late "coverage 34 ± 5" #8a5fa8 at.data [28,19] cites ["finkelstein2012"] font_size 32 hidden true
    text tx25 "25" #8f887c at.data [25,-3.5] font_size 28 hidden true
    text or_health "good health: 3.9 ± 1.5 → 13 ± 5" at.data [76,7] cites ["finkelstein2012"] font_size 30 hidden true
    text note_c "here: moved = enrolled winners" #2f6b8f x 500 y 440 font_size 36 hidden true
    scratch assume work ["1. the draw is random","2. it moves coverage","3. only through coverage","4. nobody pushed out"] x 500 y 330 font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "Dividing out the dilution"

Back to the honest comparison, winners ag (@draw mix@)ainst losers. Its twelve points come from a mix: thirty winners in a hun (@highlight mix_covered@)dred who enrolled because they won, gaining on average tau-ba (@highlight mix_uncovered@)r, and seventy who never would.
    draw m

For those seventy, winning changed nothing: no coverage, so no effect. They add zero.
    highlight m part "0.70 \\times 0" effect box

B: How do you know winning did nothing for them? They did get a letter from the state.
    point at.ref mix_uncovered gesture circle

We don't know it; we assume it. Economists call it the exclusion restriction: the draw may matter only through coverage, and a letter alone is unlikely to give anyone a clinic.
    highlight m part "0" effect box

So the whole twelve comes  (@highlight mix_covered@)from the thirty who moved.
    morph m tex "12 = 0.30 \\times {\\bar{\\tau}}"

Divide by their share: on average, forty points each, the hidden number from inside the table.
    morph m tex "{\\bar{\\tau}} = \\frac{12}{0.30} = 40"

B: So the lottery's twelve was the forty, watered down by everyone who stayed away.
    highlight m effect box

    erase mix

In general: the gap in outcomes between winners and losers, divided by the gap in coverage. It is called the LATE, the local av (@highlight m part \tau_{LATE} effect box@)erage treatment effect: the average effect for compliers, the people the draw moved.
    morph m tex "\\tau_{LATE} = \\frac{E[Y \\mid Z=1] - E[Y \\mid Z=0]}{E[D \\mid Z=1] - E[D \\mid Z=0]}"

In our example no loser got covered, so the people the draw moved are exactly the enrolled winners, and the LATE is their effect.
    draw note_c

    erase note_c

The division leans on four things: the (@draw assume_box assume_line_1@) draw is random, it moves cove (@draw assume_line_2@)rage at all, it matters only th (@draw assume_line_3@)rough coverage, and it pushes  (@draw assume_line_4@)nobody out of coverage.

    erase assume

    move m to.x 500 to.y 615 scale 0.72 duration 1.2

Now suppose the effect on the people the draw moves stayed at forty, however many it moved.
    draw ax tx0 tx25 tx50 ty20 ty40 line_eff lab_flat

Then the lottery's comparison would show forty times the extra coverage: at thirty points, twelve.
    draw rise lab_itt itt_pt readout

Grab the point and slide it along its line, up to everyone enrolling and down to almost no one, and watch the gap between the two lines.
    explore params ["s"] watch "Watch the point slide from everyone enrolling down to almost no one, and the gap between the lines."

At full take-up the two numbers meet. As take-up falls, the lottery's number sh (@highlight readout@)rinks toward zero, while the effect, held still by assumption, stays at forty.
    animate s 30 duration 2

Oregon's real numbers sit on the same picture. Some losers found Medicaid another way, so the extra coverage from winning, among th (@highlight itt_pt@)ose surveyed, was about twenty-nine points.
    animate L 34.14 s 29 duration 2.5

Winners were about ten points more likely to have a regula (@highlight or_itt@)r clinic, give or take one and a half.
    draw lab_or or_itt

Divided by twenty-nine, that is about thirty-four for those the (@highlight or_late@) lottery moved, give or take five: far too large to be chance.
    draw or_late

The same division works for how people felt: about four points more winners called their health good or better, give or take one and a half, which i (@highlight or_health@)s about thirteen for those the lottery moved, give or take five.
    draw or_health

## Which number answers the question?
voice: male
level: advanced
sources:
    - id: iche9
      title: 'ICH E9: Statistical Principles for Clinical Trials'
      authors: International Conference on Harmonisation (ICH)
      year: 1998
      finding: 'The intention-to-treat principle: the primary analysis of a confirmatory trial uses a full analysis set as close as possible to all randomized subjects, analysed as randomized.'
    - id: finkelstein2012
      title: 'The Oregon Health Insurance Experiment: Evidence from the First Year'
      authors: Finkelstein et al. (Oregon Health Study Group), Quarterly Journal of Economics
      year: 2012
      finding: Compliers were somewhat older, more likely white, in worse health and of lower socioeconomic status than the lottery population.
    - id: imbens2010
      title: 'Better LATE Than Nothing: Some Comments on Deaton (2009) and Heckman and Urzua (2009)'
      authors: Guido W. Imbens, Journal of Economic Literature
      year: 2010
      finding: 'Defends local average treatment effects: a credible estimate for a well-defined subpopulation is worth having even if it does not generalise.'
    - id: deaton2010
      title: Instruments, Randomization, and Learning about Development
      authors: Angus Deaton, Journal of Economic Literature
      year: 2010
      finding: Argues that IV and experimental estimates answer narrow questions tied to the instrument and may not tell us how a policy works elsewhere.
    - id: heckman2010
      title: 'Comparing IV with Structural Models: What Simple IV Can and Cannot Identify'
      authors: James J. Heckman and Sergio Urzua, Journal of Econometrics
      year: 2010
      finding: Argues that LATE depends on the instrument and often does not answer the policy question of interest.
takeaways:
    - 'Winners minus losers: the offer'
    - 'Enrolled vs the rest: effect + selection'
    - 'Divide by take-up: effect of coverage'

    text l_head "The offer" #4f8a4b x 290 y 630 font_size 50 hidden true
    text l_num "12" #4f8a4b x 290 y 500 font_size 130 hidden true
    text l_q1 "Run it again?" x 290 y 360 font_size 40 hidden true
    text l_q2 "Trials: as randomized" cites ["iche9"] x 290 y 270 font_size 40 hidden true
    text r_head "Coverage" #8a5fa8 x 835 y 630 font_size 50 hidden true
    text r_num "40" #8a5fa8 x 835 y 500 font_size 130 hidden true
    text r_q1 "What it does" x 835 y 360 font_size 40 hidden true
    text r_q2 "for those moved" cites ["finkelstein2012"] x 835 y 270 font_size 40 hidden true
    path divider dashed #8f887c points [[562,220],[562,680]] style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    figure board aspect 3:2 members ["l_head","l_num","l_q1","l_q2","r_head","r_num","r_q1","r_q2","divider","debate"] hidden true
    cards qs bins ["The offer: 12","Coverage: 40"] check each hidden true
        * "Run the lottery again?" bin "The offer: 12"
        * "What Medicaid does for those moved" bin "Coverage: 40"
        * "Gain per name drawn" bin "The offer: 12"
        * "Worth it for someone like them?" bin "Coverage: 40"

    card title "Which number answers the question?"

So one lottery gives two honest numbers,  (@draw divider r_head r_num@)twelve and forty. Which should a report lead with?
    draw l_head l_num

B: The forty, surely. The twelve is just the forty, damaged by people who never showed up.
    highlight l_num

Not damaged: it answers another question. Twelve is what the state gets per invitation, with take-up as people really behave.
    highlight l_head

So a state deciding whether to run its lottery again, or bud (@highlight l_num@)geting per name drawn, wants the twelve.
    draw l_q1

Forty is what coverage does for the people who take it up when off (@highlight r_num@)ered: a question about the insurance itself.
    draw r_q1

B: Then a state with better outreach could expect forty times its own take-up?
    point at.ref r_num gesture circle

Only if its new enrollees resemble Oregon's. The forty belongs to the people this lottery move (@highlight r_head@)d: poorer, sicker, and organised enough to send back the forms.
    draw r_q2

People pulled in by a harder push might gain more, or less, and this lottery cannot tell us which.
    highlight r_q2

Economists argue over such local numbers. Critics such as James Heckman and Angus Deaton say it is tied to one lottery and one group of people; Guido Imbens replies that a credible local answer beats a general guess.
    text debate "Better LATE than nothing?" cites ["imbens2010","deaton2010","heckman2010"] x 562 y 170 font_size 38

What would settle it is the effect measured at other margins: a different push that moves different people, compared with this one.
    highlight debate

Medicine chose its default long ago. The drug regulators' trial guideline, known a (@highlight l_head@)s E9, from 1998, puts the analysis by assignment first.
    draw l_q2

A pill that patients abandon helps no one, and analysing everyone as randomized keeps the comparison fair.
    highlight l_q2

    erase l_q1 l_q2 r_q1 r_q2 divider l_head l_num r_head r_num debate

Try it on four questions people actually ask.
    draw qs

    ask "Which number answers each question? Drag each card to its number." on qs store f after keep
        right: All four: questions about the program want the twelve, questions about insurance want the forty.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} on the first try.

Questions about the policy as delivered want the twelve. Questions about what insurance does want the forty, and only for someone like the people the lottery moved.
    highlight qs

    erase qs

    quiz "A clinic offers free nicotine patches to a random half of its smokers; nobody else gets them. Only half of those offered use them. A year on, 20% of those offered have quit, against 14% of the rest. What did the patches do for those who used them?" intro "One new trial." voice b
        right: About twelve: the six-point gap from the offer, divided by the half who actually used the patches.
        wrong: The six points are spread over everyone offered. Ask how many of them actually wore a patch.
        * About 6 points more quitting
        + About 12 points more quitting
        * About 3 points more quitting

So what does comparing Oregon's winners with its losers measure? The effect of being offered Medicaid: honest, and diluted.

Splitting the winners by who enrolled throws the lottery away, because enrolling was a choice.

And dividing by the gap in coverage brings back the effect of insurance itself, for the people the lottery moved.

The twelve was never wrong. It answers the question of whoever sends the letters.
    highlight takeaway_1

